KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ MD->MS then conversion-operator readback: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
^.*is unknown.*$
--
[over.match.conv] with [temp.deduct]/8: an instantiated template conversion
function is not a viable *non-template* conversion-operator candidate; failing
to form its call is a soft non-viability handled by fresh template deduction,
not a hard error.

KNOWNBUG: for mutually-convertible class types each carrying a templated
conversion operator, deducing a conversion (e.g. MD -> int) instantiates the
source class's `operator(signed_int)` as a class component.  That instance is
not registered in the class cpp_scope for name lookup, yet the non-template
branch of user_defined_conversion_sequence iterated it and built a name-driven
member call, throwing "symbol 'operator(signed_int)' is unknown" and aborting
the enclosing statement (so the assertion is silently dropped -> vacuous
SUCCESS).

Minimal model of libstdc++'s std::ranges::__detail::__max_size_type /
__max_diff_type.  Reclassify CORE once the non-template branch treats an
unresolvable instantiated conversion operator as a soft skip.
